\title{RSModule: Base Class for Singleton Utility Modules}
\description{
\code{RSModule} is a structural base class used to define immutable, singleton-accessed modules
in the RS framework. It is intended for shared read-only utilities, configurations, or
stateless service objects that are initialized once and reused throughout runtime.

This class enforces singleton behavior: calling \verb{$new()} directly raises an error if
the instance already exists. Subclasses must define their own \verb{$module()} constructor
to return the singleton instance.

Unlike \code{RSSingleton}, this class assumes immutability after initialization. All fields
must be defined before calling \code{super$initialize()}, after which all bindings are locked.
Subclasses may define public instance properties and methods, but all properties
must be defined before calling \code{super$initialize()}, as the module instance will be locked
against any further modification or shape changes.
}
\note{
It is recommended to lock the \verb{$instance} slot after first instantiation
using \code{lockBinding("instance", YourModuleClass)}.
}
\section{Usage}{

\itemize{
\item Define a subclass that inherits \code{RSModule}
\item Populate instance fields in \code{initialize()}
\item Call \code{super$initialize()} last
\item Define a \verb{$module()} method returning a singleton instance
}
}

\section{Methods}{

\itemize{
\item \code{initialize()} — Guards against double instantiation
\item \code{lockVariables()} — Locks all bindings to enforce immutability
}
}

